Subject: A faster lift with the same output

Profiling a lift showed most of its time going to repeated work. The disassembler copied a section's
bytes for every function it read (35,000 times) and re-sorted every known address after each new one
(787 times); the bytes are now read once per section and the addresses kept sorted as they arrive.
The per-section assembly listings, which no later step reads, are not written with --no-listings.
The recompiler decoded each instruction with Capstone's detail once per function and pass that covered
it, 7.5 million decodes for the game's 24,000 functions. It now walks instruction streams with
Capstone's light decode and reads an instruction's detail once per address and encoding, sharing the
result, since nothing changes an instruction once made. The generated C is the same, byte for byte.

